title: No POST /auth - registry credentials cannot be verified
updated: 2026-08-27T06:34:44Z
---

System has info, version, ping, events and df, but not POST /auth.

The endpoint checks a set of registry credentials against the registry and
answers 200 with {"Status":"Login Succeeded"} or 401. It takes the same
AuthConfig body that push encodes into X-Registry-Auth, so
_build_registry_auth_header already builds most of what is needed.

Consumer motivation: ../p5-dist-zilla-plugin-docker-api pushes release
images and currently discovers a bad or expired credential only when the
push fails, halfway through a release, after the image is already built and
tagged. A cheap pre-flight check would let it fail before doing the work.

Suggested shape:
  $docker->system->auth(
    username => ..., password => ..., serveraddress => ...,
  );
or accept the same auth argument images->push takes, so a caller can hand
over whatever it was going to push with.

2026-08-27 05:20 Implemented as API::Docker::API::System/auth.

  $docker->system->auth(username => ..., password => ..., serveraddress => ...)
  $docker->system->auth(auth => $config)   # same argument images->push takes

Both spellings, never mixed: the AuthConfig has no "auth" field so there is no
ambiguity, but passing both croaks rather than silently picking a set of
credentials. auth => accepts a HashRef, a JSON object, or an already-encoded
X-Registry-Auth value, which is decoded back through API::Docker::Role::RegistryAuth.

Returns the decoded engine answer ({Status, IdentityToken}); a rejected
credential croaks, which is what makes it usable as a pre-flight check.
response => \%res is passed through so an eval-ing caller reaches the status.
Passing no credentials at all croaks before the request -- stricter than the
engine on purpose.

Measured against rootless Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41): the endpoint exists, but a
failed check is 500, not Docker 401, with the registry text wrapped by Podman;
an empty AuthConfig answers 500 "cannot prompt for username without stdin".
Documented -- test the outcome, not the status number. No login was ever
performed: probes used an unreachable serveraddress (127.0.0.1:1).

Tests: t/system_auth.t (fixture-driven, no socket). prove -lr t/ and dzil test
